; bfsodium CSHAKE256 : NIST SP 800_185 section 3's cSHAKE at SHAKE256's rate
;
; NOTE square brackets are brainfuck loops  so comments use braces for counts;
;
; HAND WRITTEN skeleton; keccak/bytepad136 is PASTED to build the block that
; goes in front of the message  and keccak/sponge136 is PASTED to absorb it;
; This file adds no cryptography of its own  which is the point of it;
;
; IO  in:  nlen{2} LE  slen{2} LE  mlen{2} LE  olen{2} LE  nbuf{128}
;          sbuf{128}  then mlen bytes from the wire
;     out: olen bytes
;
;   cSHAKE(X  L  N  S) = KECCAK at the rate of( bytepad( encode_string(N)
;                        then encode_string(S)  rate ) then X then 00  L )
;
; which is the sponge this library already has  over a message that begins on
; the TAPE and continues on the WIRE; keccak/sponge136 grew a tape prefix
; for exactly this  so the whole of cSHAKE here is: build the block  stage it
; where the sponge looks for its prefix  and hand over the padding byte;
;
; THE PADDING BYTE IS 4 AND NOT 31  and that one cell is the whole difference
; between this file and SHAKE256; FIPS 202 gives the SHA3 functions 6 and the
; SHAKEs 31  and SP 800_185 gives the customizable pair 4; keccak/sponge136's
; header said so before this file existed;
;
; AND WITH NO NAME AND NO CUSTOMIZATION STRING cSHAKE IS SHAKE  which the
; standard states outright rather than leaving to arithmetic: it is NOT the
; prefix construction with two empty strings  which would absorb a whole block
; of encodings and give a different answer; So both lengths being nought is a
; BRANCH  and it clears the staged prefix and puts 31 back in the padding cell;
; The block is built either way  because building it costs a few hundred
; thousand instructions against the permutation's two and a half thousand
; million  and a branch around it would cost more to read than to run;
;
; NLEN PLUS SLEN MUST BE AT MOST 128  which is keccak/bytepad136's limit
; and not this file's; it is restated here because a caller reads this file
; and not that one;
;
; TAPE MAP  (home @0)
;   @0x000        pad     the padding byte the sponge is handed; 4 or 31
;   @0x001:0x002  mlen{2} the message length  little endian
;   @0x003:0x004  olen{2} the output length
;   @0x005:0x006  plen{2} how much prefix the sponge takes from the tape
;   @0x007:0x08e  the sponge's own low staging area for that prefix; this file
;                 puts the block there and the sponge's body moves it up
;   @0x000:0x637  keccak/sponge136 pasted at this file's own zero
;   @0x640:0x803  keccak/bytepad136's frame  pasted at @0x640; its two
;                 lengths at @0x640 and @0x642  its two buffers at @0x644
;                 and @0x6c4  and its answer at @0x748
;   @0x808:0x80b  mlen and olen parked while bytepad runs  because the cells
;                 they arrive in are inside the sponge's own frame
;   @0x80c        the flag that says both strings are empty
;   @0x80d:0x80e    the cells the lengths are tested through
;
; THE TWO PASTED FRAMES DO NOT OVERLAP  and that is why this works: the sponge
; owns nought to 0x637 and bytepad sits above it at @0x640  so the block can be
; built first and moved down into the sponge's staging area afterwards;

; read the four lengths and the two strings
,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,>,
